Palindromic Partitioning – Minimum Cuts

This is a HIGH-LEVEL DP problem asked in:

MAANG interviews

Advanced coding rounds

Competitive programming

🎯 Aim

To partition a given string into substrings such that:

Every substring is a palindrome

The number of cuts is minimum

🔍 Problem Statement

Given a string s, return the minimum number of cuts needed so that every substring of the partition is a palindrome.

Example
Input:  s = "aab"
Output: 1


Explanation

"a | ab" ❌
"aa | b" ✅   → only 1 cut

🧠 Key Observation

If the entire string is already a palindrome → 0 cuts

Bruteforce is exponential ❌

Dynamic Programming is required ✅

⚙️ Approach

We use DP + Palindrome Precomputation

Two DP arrays:

isPal[i][j] → checks if substring s[i..j] is palindrome

dp[i] → minimum cuts needed for substring s[0..i]

🪜 Algorithm
Step 1: Precompute Palindromes

Fill isPal[][] using gap technique

Step 2: Compute Minimum Cuts

If s[0..i] is palindrome → dp[i] = 0

Else:

dp[i] = min(dp[j - 1] + 1)
where s[j..i] is palindrome

💻 Java Code (Industry-Level, Clean)
class PalindromicPartitioning {

    public static int minCut(String s) {
        int n = s.length();

        // Step 1: Palindrome table
        boolean[][] isPal = new boolean[n][n];

        // Gap strategy
        for (int g = 0; g < n; g++) {
            for (int i = 0, j = g; j < n; i++, j++) {
                if (g == 0)
                    isPal[i][j] = true;
                else if (g == 1)
                    isPal[i][j] = (s.charAt(i) == s.charAt(j));
                else
                    isPal[i][j] =
                            (s.charAt(i) == s.charAt(j)) && isPal[i + 1][j - 1];
            }
        }

        // Step 2: DP for min cuts
        int[] dp = new int[n];

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {

            // If whole substring is palindrome
            if (isPal[0][i]) {
                dp[i] = 0;
            } else {
                int min = Integer.MAX_VALUE;

                for (int j = i; j >= 1; j--) {
                    if (isPal[j][i]) {
                        min = Math.min(min, dp[j - 1] + 1);
                    }
                }
                dp[i] = min;
            }
        }

        return dp[n - 1];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(minCut("aab"));
    }
}

🧾 Output
1

🪜 Line-by-Line Explanation (Very Important)
🔹 boolean[][] isPal

Stores whether s[i..j] is a palindrome.

🔹 Gap Strategy
for (int g = 0; g < n; g++)


g = 0 → single characters (always palindrome)

g = 1 → two characters

g > 1 → check ends + inner substring

🔹 Main DP Array
int[] dp = new int[n];


dp[i] → min cuts for string 0..i

🔹 If no cut needed
if (isPal[0][i]) dp[i] = 0;

🔹 Trying all partitions
min = Math.min(min, dp[j - 1] + 1);


Cut before index j

Add 1 cut

Choose minimum

